   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.9",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Multiple Representations of Limits",
    "stem": "The function f is defined by f(x) = (x² − 9)/(x − 3) for x ≠ 3, and f(3) = 2. A table gives these values: at x = 2.9, f(x) = 5.9; at x = 2.99, f(x) = 5.99; at x = 3.01, f(x) = 6.01; at x = 3.1, f(x) = 6.1. Which statement is consistent with both the formula and the table?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim x→3 f(x) = 2, because f(3) = 2.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This matches the limit to the function value instead of to the approach behavior. Nearby values cluster around 6 in both the table and the simplified formula, not around 2. Fix: Read the table entries on each side of x = 3 and ask what value they approach."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "lim x→3 f(x) does not exist, because substituting x = 3 into the formula gives 0/0.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats the indeterminate form 0/0 as proof that no limit exists. A 0/0 result only signals that more work is needed; factoring gives x + 3, whose limit at 3 is 6. Fix: When substitution gives 0/0, factor and cancel, then evaluate the simplified expression."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim x→3 f(x) does not exist, because f(3) = 2 does not match the trend in the table.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses a limit failing to exist with a function being discontinuous. A mismatch between f(3) and the trend makes f discontinuous at 3, but both one-sided limits equal 6, so the limit exists. Fix: Compare the left and right approach values to each other, not to f(3)."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "lim x→3 f(x) = 6, even though f(3) = 2.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "For x ≠ 3 the formula simplifies to x + 3, which approaches 6 as x approaches 3. The table shows values approaching 6 from both sides. The limit describes approach behavior, so the separately assigned value f(3) = 2 does not affect it."
     }
    ]
   },
